==Dutch==
{{wp|lang=nl}}

===Etymology===
According to the ''[[w:Woordenboek der Nederlandsche Taal|Woordenboek der Nederlandsche Taal]]'', the term is an evolution of the now obsolete interjections ''amij'', ''amy'' or ''ammy'' (formed from ''ah'' and the personal pronoun ''[[mij]]''; compare also {{cog|fr|à moi}}), as an exclamation of sorrow or indignation, pain, aversion, pity, etc.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|nl|/aːˈmɑi̯/}}
* {{audio|nl|Nl-amai.ogg}}
* {{hyphenation|nl|amai}}

===Interjection===
{{head|nl|interjection}}

# {{lb|nl|Belgium|Zeelandic Flanders}} exclamation of surprise or disappointment; [[boy]]!
#: {{uxi|nl|'''Amai''', [[wat|wa']][[een|ne]] [[klap]]!|'''[[jee|Jee]]''', what a [[punch]] (or [[thunderstroke]])}}
#: {{uxi|nl|Wa'ne [[stoot]], '''amai'''!|What a [[blunder]], '''gosh'''!}}
# {{lb|nl|Belgium|Zeelandic Flanders}} [[oh my]], [[amazing]]
#: {{uxi|nl|'''Amai''', da'ziet er goed uit!|'''Oh my''', that is looking good!}}

